implementationPlan.description:
RED first (load test-driven-development).

The cap contract is shared with the daemon row task-91ef7886, the proxy row task-685d8a8e and JetBrains task-4f1e37f3:
- 64 KiB is 65536 bytes;
- read at most 65537 bytes through one descriptor;
- a larger file counts as unreadable.

The tests pin 65536 and 65537 as literals. Build each fixture as JSON.stringify(info) padded with trailing spaces to an exact byte length (assert Buffer.byteLength equals it). JSON allows trailing whitespace.

SAFETY: do NOT add a /dev/zero case, or any other never-ending device. Today's fs.readFileSync of one never reaches EOF and grows memory until the process dies; a synchronous read cannot be stopped by the vitest timeout. The daemon row's RED run was killed with SIGTERM that way (mem:gotcha-dev-zero-red-run-runaway). Case (2)'s exact read total proves the bound without one.

NEVER run npm run build, npm run clean, npm ci or npm install in the shared checkout's packages/moe-claude-plugin: the hook shim hooks/post-tool-use.mjs loads its dist, and its node_modules is shared. All runs happen in the step 3 snapshot.

packages/moe-claude-plugin/tests/daemonClient.test.ts: add describe('daemon.json size cap'). Each case uses its own mkdtemp project, removed in afterEach together with vi.restoreAllMocks().

(1) Boundary.
- A valid 65536-byte daemon.json: findDaemonInfo({ projectPath }) toEqual({ port, pid, startedAt, projectPath, host: '127.0.0.1' }).
- The 65537-byte version throws a DaemonError whose code is 'DAEMON_NOT_FOUND'. Capture it with try/catch and assert instanceof and code.

(2) Bounded read. Spy, pass-through, on fs.readFileSync, fs.openSync, fs.readSync and fs.closeSync. daemonClient.ts and the test both use the default import of node:fs, so the spies see its calls; this was verified in a snapshot. Write an 8 MiB daemon.json: valid JSON padded with spaces. Assert:
- DAEMON_NOT_FOUND is thrown;
- readFileSync is never called with that daemon.json path;
- openSync opened that path exactly once;
- the readSync return values for the descriptor openSync returned sum to EXACTLY 65537. Not "at most": a size pre-check that never reads gives 0, and an unbounded read gives more;
- closeSync received that descriptor exactly once.

(3) Error path. Mock fs.readSync to throw an Error whose code is 'EIO', for a valid small daemon.json. Assert:
- DAEMON_NOT_FOUND is thrown, so no other error escapes;
- closeSync still received the descriptor openSync returned.

packages/moe-claude-plugin/tests/postToolUse.test.ts, inside describe('runPostToolUseHook'). Its beforeEach already clears MOE_PROJECT_PATH and starts the WebSocket server.

(4) Fail-open. A project whose daemon.json is 65537 bytes of valid JSON pointing at the live test server's port, with projectPath = the project. Set MOE_WORKER_ID = 'worker-test' and spy process.stderr.write with mockImplementation(() => true). Then run runPostToolUseHook({ tool_name: 'mcp__moe__moe_list_tasks', session_id: 's5', cwd: project }). Assert:
- the call resolves, with no throw, to exactly { forwarded: false, reason: 'send-failed' };
- after a 100 ms wait, received has length 0;
- the captured stderr contains 'Moe daemon not running for project', which is the existing DAEMON_NOT_FOUND handling.
Restore MOE_WORKER_ID and the spy in finally, like the sibling cases.

In the snapshot, the 65537 case of (1), (2), (3) and (4) fail on today's code (today's readFileSync parses the file and (4) forwards), while the 65536 case and the existing 13 tests pass. Record that in the step note.

Adversarial self-review (load adversarial-self-review) of the three-file diff. Check:
- the off-by-one at the cap: 65536 parses, 65537 is refused;
- short reads are looped;
- the descriptor is closed on every path, including a throwing readSync;
- no whole-file read of daemon.json remains (grep readFileSync in src: only readRecentProject's projects.json read may remain);
- the stderr line carries no file content;
- every new test restores its spies and env vars and removes its temp dir, even on failure;
- valid daemon.json files resolve exactly as before (the 13 existing tests are untouched).

Residuals, noted only and pre-existing:
- like a malformed file today, an oversize daemon.json in the pinned project lets the walk-up continue to an ancestor's daemon.json (walk-up discovery is out of scope);
- opening a FIFO blocks, but git cannot commit FIFOs.

If the review changes bytes, re-run the step 3 gate fresh. Report all three paths in complete_step.modifiedFiles.
